#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082923 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf1d89ed-0d2a-5759-9e6e-354957f7dabc" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Improper Integral of the Second Kind"}] :question/id #uuid "70734fff-e45d-5b6d-9643-9e94dbcd2fcb" :question/math-academy-id "e-517" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\tan x\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u = \\cos x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -\\sin x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\textrm d u = \\sin x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}\\frac{-\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u) = \\dfrac{1}{u} \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad\\textrm{ as }\\quad u\\rightarrow 0^+.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to0^+.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u||}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[0-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|] \\\\  & =-(-\\infty ) \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083016 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1894719c-d398-515d-9a7b-6af74727106c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Improper Integrals of the Second Kind"}] :question/id #uuid "8853c8c3-821c-5ae5-9817-ae8e6d7a55a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-7297" :question/problem "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{x-2}\\qquad$  II. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{0} \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{x+2}\\qquad$  III. $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{3} \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{x^2-1}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its upper limit is infinite. So, it is not an improper integral of the second kind.\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x+2} \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\rightarrow (-2)^+.\n$$\n\n- Integral III is not an improper integral since it has finite limits, and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in[2,3].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083059 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83d52a4-6f69-5607-8753-e96c13bd7209" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting One of the Limits of an Improper Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "201e4ce6-d718-5cd1-ba7a-851accf314ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-9959" :question/problem "Rewrite the improper integral $\\displaystyle {\\int_{0}^{1} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x^2-1}}$ as the limit of definite integral." :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (0,{\\color{blue}{1}})\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf(x) =   \\dfrac {1}{x^2-1} \\rightarrow -\\infty\\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\rightarrow {\\color{blue}{1}} ^-\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [0, b]$ and taking the limit as $b\\to{\\color{blue}1}^-\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int_{0}^{1} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x^2-1}} = \\lim\\limits_{b \\to {\\color{blue}{1}}^-} \\left( \\displaystyle {\\int_{0}^{b} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x^2-1}}   \\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1894719c-d398-515d-9a7b-6af74727106c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Improper Integrals of the Second Kind"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92a68b38-f5e7-5b04-bedd-97ac1661d2f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328816} {:answer/id #uuid "0958cc90-ff3a-532f-93b7-3ac06107821c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328817} {:answer/id #uuid "333ea59c-7e9d-5933-8b07-c020617fe5d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328818} {:answer/id #uuid "d48a96cb-f426-514e-8621-22b25725fe36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328819} {:answer/id #uuid "b204cedf-1458-5082-b75b-bfc78e4a714c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "333ea59c-7e9d-5933-8b07-c020617fe5d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328818} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbb3c69d-810f-5c22-91f7-a6ea8e75e9c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c5f2100-5d65-54c0-a496-a5ee04c005c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-115206" :question/problem "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its lower limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral III is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4}\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-2)^{+}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf1d89ed-0d2a-5759-9e6e-354957f7dabc" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Improper Integral of the Second Kind"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5df580a9-0eaa-5ae1-82df-bdaf3844274d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328823} {:answer/id #uuid "a2333b34-e8b2-5506-ac82-caedc47e5776" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328824} {:answer/id #uuid "022ddb0f-863d-56b7-b9b2-ae84f2558c65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328825} {:answer/id #uuid "8ffd87f0-df5a-5032-bbb5-b4b9f2b5e83a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328826} {:answer/id #uuid "55354bac-7dbf-5020-91fd-d329bffbc266" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5df580a9-0eaa-5ae1-82df-bdaf3844274d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328823} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e2cd14d-b1e9-5fa5-9bc6-9332fff67489" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "06043800-b433-5131-a08a-adba5684db0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115353" :question/problem "${\\int }_{2/3}^{2}\\frac{3}{(3x-2)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=3x-2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $4$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2/3}^{2}\\frac{3\\,\\text{d}x}{(3x-2)^{5}} & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{5}} & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{4}{u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{(-\\frac{1}{4}\\,{u}^{-4})|}_{a}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}|}_{a}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\frac{1}{256}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}] \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83d52a4-6f69-5607-8753-e96c13bd7209" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting One of the Limits of an Improper Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9248410-e441-5c03-a317-5057ddd33e3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to {4}^{-}}({\\int }_{0}^{b}\\frac{1}{16-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328830} {:answer/id #uuid "fb395732-74e6-5076-b471-849d7a9912de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to {0}^{-}}({\\int }_{-4}^{b}\\frac{1}{16-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328831} {:answer/id #uuid "0ad28b0d-d3e2-5ebb-805b-cd0f3aab5a6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to {0}^{+}}({\\int }_{b}^{4}\\frac{1}{16-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328832} {:answer/id #uuid "1cac2c6c-3348-5090-86cb-04404f1c8831" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to -\\infty }({\\int }_{b}^{4}\\frac{1}{16-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328833} {:answer/id #uuid "3e632bcc-3bb7-5f70-ae14-9a9a0e1f1fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }({\\int }_{0}^{b}\\frac{1}{16-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9248410-e441-5c03-a317-5057ddd33e3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to {4}^{-}}({\\int }_{0}^{b}\\frac{1}{16-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328830} :answer-field/id #uuid "326cc452-8559-526f-9a46-67c0b1326729" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03bdf3c9-a985-5a7c-8f5b-a9c50ed62e1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-141131" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to \n${\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{16-{x}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (0,4):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{16-{x}^{2}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {4}^{-}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [0,b]$ and taking the limit as $b\\to {4}^{-}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{16-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to {4}^{-}}({\\int }_{0}^{b}\\frac{1}{16-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328835 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1894719c-d398-515d-9a7b-6af74727106c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Improper Integrals of the Second Kind"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b0558cd-f3d4-57d8-a64c-4d008be2e5ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328837} {:answer/id #uuid "6b5b7e13-ae9d-5c23-8fc2-19ae91bea3fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328838} {:answer/id #uuid "a877658d-d8c1-5ff7-9e95-226fa7d4f19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328839} {:answer/id #uuid "df53ae90-26e1-55e9-8962-66e5cf64019a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328840} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc526ad-62ec-52cd-9cfd-1ab35260d272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fc526ad-62ec-52cd-9cfd-1ab35260d272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db