/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328847} {:answer/id #uuid "7f5685de-a25d-5382-b4c5-dbe511ccc756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff060d12-ad85-5749-b4fb-d2a2fc498ea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328847} :answer-field/id #uuid "2216ef81-487b-5f29-b6c5-a2761b853d78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "529291bc-02f3-50cf-9553-071e1b8277ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-141212" :question/problem "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{x}-1}\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{1-\\sqrt{x}}\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{x-1}}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its upper limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral II is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [2,4].$\n\n- Integral III is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {1}^{+}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328849 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1894719c-d398-515d-9a7b-6af74727106c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Improper Integrals of the Second Kind"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3a1bd5a-1e05-5b35-a087-026c604901c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328851} {:answer/id #uuid "d4acc7e3-7c5f-52b2-a1b9-780e10358e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328852} {:answer/id #uuid "0cbfbe65-07ab-5f42-bddb-3ef1d8cc3633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328853} {:answer/id #uuid "c3fd97f7-4d68-59db-9aa2-027c7ea7ade4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328854} {:answer/id #uuid "f2714f98-9172-5ead-b98d-279127b54709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4acc7e3-7c5f-52b2-a1b9-780e10358e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328852} :answer-field/id #uuid "f76abcdf-9ff5-5ece-aa1d-f6c940cae9c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fbe5b7e3-d8d6-5458-b65a-c22b37902e9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-141218" :question/problem "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\infty }\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {0}^{-}.\n$$\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its upper limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral III is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328856 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83d52a4-6f69-5607-8753-e96c13bd7209" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting One of the Limits of an Improper Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da9e539b-a122-5e1a-b231-ef969843f136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to +\\infty }({\\int }_{a}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328858} {:answer/id #uuid "c3da3169-5260-58f9-83dc-ad6f2abe447a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to {1}^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328859} {:answer/id #uuid "87ce5447-a40a-50f7-89c2-420c2e509c8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\int }_{a}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328860} {:answer/id #uuid "e506dbe8-40cc-53a8-bfde-5051634181d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to {2}^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328861} {:answer/id #uuid "a43817c5-4c5b-50eb-95dd-c9c21753d221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to {4}^{+}}({\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3da3169-5260-58f9-83dc-ad6f2abe447a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to {1}^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328859} :answer-field/id #uuid "79ae6b55-2d9d-57ff-9a87-83aba80eb62c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4cf32d39-29ef-5107-b7e8-3a543d985a4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-142389" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to \n${\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}?$" :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (1,4):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {1}^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,4]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to {1}^{+}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to {1}^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83d52a4-6f69-5607-8753-e96c13bd7209" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting One of the Limits of an Improper Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2774264a-0623-5b4e-80a0-1eb17f87b7b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to +\\infty }({\\int }_{-1}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328865} {:answer/id #uuid "2acc239a-b8e9-5996-8e79-066ff266badb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to (-1)^{-}}({\\int }_{b}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328866} {:answer/id #uuid "872fba4f-8f15-51db-8219-37f027575c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to -\\infty }({\\int }_{b}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328867} {:answer/id #uuid "d0017260-670d-531d-9506-2b727ac2370c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to {25}^{-}}({\\int }_{-1}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328868} {:answer/id #uuid "6a774773-dc17-5e6d-87d7-443fca63aec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to {5}^{-}}({\\int }_{-1}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a774773-dc17-5e6d-87d7-443fca63aec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{b\\to {5}^{-}}({\\int }_{-1}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328869} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e6b8d84-a3e0-55b9-a5af-146925a95079" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9bba3045-3b2b-590f-bed1-ad9f6f458f1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-142390" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to \n${\\int }_{-1}^{5}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}?$" :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-1,5):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {5}^{-}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [-1,b]$ and taking the limit as $b\\to {5}^{-}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to {5}^{-}}({\\int }_{-1}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328870 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf1d89ed-0d2a-5759-9e6e-354957f7dabc" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Improper Integral of the Second Kind"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bd7fe64-d0d0-5327-9057-c42c762ba23c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bd7fe64-d0d0-5327-9057-c42c762ba23c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328872} :answer-field/id #uuid "071028ed-551a-5c50-a5d1-3a22d871c65c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1dc9e4a0-dba4-54bb-8093-9583c6a7e4ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-351487" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt[5]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt[5]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)={u}^{-1/5}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\frac{5}{4}{u}^{4/5}|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {1}^{4/5}-\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {a}^{4/5}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt[5]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83d52a4-6f69-5607-8753-e96c13bd7209" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting One of the Limits of an Improper Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e5bf982-f1c8-5150-ab37-0ba07f03e692" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328875} {:answer/id #uuid "250572b8-fd81-5587-9309-1e21d3354b64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to (-1)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328876} {:answer/id #uuid "92688bfd-0d4d-5819-91d9-d4c1ff75b5ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328877} {:answer/id #uuid "4809118f-446a-560f-a07b-4a0ac0f8b5bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to {0}^{-}}({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328878} {:answer/id #uuid "09d1d397-fb03-5b92-ba8e-b40d728f29bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to +\\infty }({\\int }_{-1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "250572b8-fd81-5587-9309-1e21d3354b64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to (-1)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328876} :answer-field/id #uuid "26279a06-072f-57ea-b112-9ea83dd49ae1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ec7d7d3-2155-5526-8dab-037eb77edfa2" :question/math-academy-id "q-49889" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to \n${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{3}+1}?$" :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-1,1):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-1)^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,1]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to (-1)^{+}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to (-1)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf1d89ed-0d2a-5759-9e6e-354957f7dabc" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Improper Integral of the Second Kind"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd9c2f8f-8f29-5566-8ddf-6aaa86b24d4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328882} {:answer/id #uuid "c281505a-9e30-59a4-a596-e0a321c18948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328883} {:answer/id #uuid "e685dcce-5e98-5680-a324-25efdf276b3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328884} {:answer/id #uuid "9920d468-65c0-557b-a25c-5a879c915609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328885} {:answer/id #uuid "9dbd8a42-7d17-539f-b8a6-d76cc4a649af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dbd8a42-7d17-539f-b8a6-d76cc4a649af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328886} :answer-field/id #uuid "abd31044-ca4b-5c77-8e41-f2c01b39f93e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b8978839-8740-53c7-a939-fdd329cbc12b" :question/math-academy-id "q-49894" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {0}^{+}\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}4{x}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}5{x}^{4/5}{|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}{|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[5\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{{1}^{4}}-5\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{{a}^{4}}] \\\\  & =5(1)-5(0) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1894719c-d398-515d-9a7b-6af74727106c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Improper Integrals of the Second Kind"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56765ab1-02a2-549d-9e77-901916deb7a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328889} {:answer/id #uuid "be34cb41-e7b0-53c7-89e7-b6abc4485ef1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328890} {:answer/id #uuid "562c517a-34ef-5338-b999-9ea424994c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328891} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6b0d12-4032-5952-8020-356c379b88e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328892} {:answer/id #uuid "294875ea-c563-58ee-b0b5-57da05e599ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "562c517a-34ef-5338-b999-9ea424994c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328891} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f11c304-a9c8-54f6-9a46-9dadf5e273a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f90240a-5868-5807-86ec-d1870a9e50ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-49928" :question/problem "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-9}\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-9}\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-9}\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {3}^{-}.\n$$\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its lower limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral III is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [1,3].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083745 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf1d89ed-0d2a-5759-9e6e-354957f7dabc" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Improper Integral of the Second Kind"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3bd43b9-db45-531a-9755-66e46f4a06f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081837} {:answer/id #uuid "0bc7e659-854f-5c5d-a1d3-95b8235f0e12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081838} {:answer/id #uuid "16da06da-5da7-52f1-92fd-6c04b286a5fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3} - 2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081839} {:answer/id #uuid "4c401ee5-810e-54f9-9219-b3106bb36507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081840} {:answer/id #uuid "59f2a479-427c-53f9-98c6-db51dc6d5ecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3bd43b9-db45-531a-9755-66e46f4a06f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081837} :answer-field/id #uuid "1211dc12-9cc3-54a6-977d-9d23eed867d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "85684966-d150-5a31-a0f4-6b8b903f342d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6039" :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{4-t}}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=4-t.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=-1\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{4-t}} & ={\\int }_{3}^{0}\\frac{-\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{3}^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{u}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{u}} & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{3}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{2{u}^{1/2}|}_{a}^{3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{2\\sqrt{u}|}_{a}^{3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[2\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{a}] \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}-0 \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{4-t}}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83d52a4-6f69-5607-8753-e96c13bd7209" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting One of the Limits of an Improper Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "983bebe8-2cff-56a8-b21f-4aa9b9eb5e95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to {3}^{-}}({\\int }_{-2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328896} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2a811b-d2f5-53eb-a37e-9e3e1213b050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\int }_{a}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328897} {:answer/id #uuid "6bc5b648-ec1a-5418-acf2-3a8ed5617a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to (-2)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328898} {:answer/id #uuid "328179f1-7e77-5d6e-8a3f-4b09ccdf9a2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({\\int }_{-2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328899} {:answer/id #uuid "0896a1bc-34be-588d-bd8a-161782bca4a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to (-8)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bc5b648-ec1a-5418-acf2-3a8ed5617a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to (-2)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328898} :answer-field/id #uuid "310f1fc3-5a8e-5642-a464-03f8d607049e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2da8aa5-7ca5-5be0-be72-5709a0d5972c" :question/math-academy-id "q-82701" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to \n${\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{3}+8}?$" :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-2,3):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-2)^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,3]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to (-2)^{+}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to (-2)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328901 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf1d89ed-0d2a-5759-9e6e-354957f7dabc" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Improper Integral of the Second Kind"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e4e806c-26c8-51d6-8798-f36eb0f6abe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328903} {:answer/id #uuid "247087b3-7a64-5cb7-9d22-ccfb3696c981" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328904} {:answer/id #uuid "3014329a-929d-5e94-b59a-33a8f4d7d3fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328905} {:answer/id #uuid "37be8d72-636a-588b-9a28-dd66200a86ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328906} {:answer/id #uuid "7af2cabc-9776-5c72-bfe0-e8e1ebb63898" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3014329a-929d-5e94-b59a-33a8f4d7d3fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328905} :answer-field/id #uuid "089f1ec9-ba3d-55b3-8ad0-925a7d01e899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f8b525b2-3937-540a-90ac-044fa724bc45" :question/math-academy-id "q-82842" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral  ${\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=x-2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\frac{1}{u}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u||}_{a}^{2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2}{a}| \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent."}]}
